findings.message:
Assumption of Fixed-Length Array Identified.

VULNERABILITY: Assumption of Fixed-Length Array via uint8 Loop Counter
'''
The `Vault` contract uses a `uint8` loop counter to iterate over dynamic `uint256[]` arrays in two internal functions:

1. `_withdraw(address _to, uint256[] memory _ids, bool _force)`:
   ```solidity
   for (uint8 i = 0; i < _ids.length; i++) {
       amount += _withdrawDeposit(_ids[i], localTotalShares, localTotalPrincipal, _to, _force);
   }
   ```

2. `_unsponsor(address _to, uint256[] memory _ids, bool _force)`:
   ```solidity
   for (uint8 i = 0; i < _ids.length; i++) {
       uint256 tokenId = _ids[i];
       // ... processing ...
   }
   ```

These loops implicitly assume that the dynamic array length will never exceed the maximum value of `uint8` (255). If a caller provides an array with more than 255 elements, the increment `i++` will overflow when `i` reaches 255. Because the contract uses Solidity 0.8.10, arithmetic overflow is checked and will revert the entire transaction. This means any attempt to withdraw or unsponsor more than 255 deposits in a single call will fail, even though the array type itself supports arbitrary length.

This is a logic error caused by an implicit fixed-length assumption (the counter type) applied to a dynamic array. While the caller could split the operation into multiple transactions, the contract does not handle dynamic array lengths properly beyond 255, leading to a denial-of-service for that transaction and potential usability issues for users with many deposits.

Recommended mitigation/fix (no upgrade code required):
Change the loop counter type from `uint8` to `uint256` in both functions. For example:
```solidity
for (uint256 i = 0; i < _ids.length; i++) {
    // ...
}
```
This correctly handles any dynamic array length and removes the unintended 255-element limit.
'''
Detector: DataCorruptionDetector
Classification: logic (no standard SWC)
